# the shape of it — positioning, properties, forecast
# -------------------------------------------------

# - c:    trust the programmer; minimal abstractions; manual everything.
# - rust: never trust the programmer; the compiler proves memory safety;
#         rich type system.
# - zig:  trust the programmer, but give them better tools; no hidden
#         control flow, no hidden allocations, no hidden i/o.
#
#                  C            Zig          Rust
# abstraction     low           low          high
# safety          manual        manual+      compile-proven
# ecosystem       enormous      tiny         large
# learning curve  medium        low          steep
#
# zig is "c done right in 2026" — NOT "rust done lighter". that is the whole
# distinction. zig is the c person's UPGRADE PATH; rust is the c person's
# ESCAPE HATCH.

# FORECAST: zig replaces neither c nor rust.
# - rust keeps the security-critical slot it is already winning — kernels,
#   browsers, crypto, network parsers, cloud infra.
# - zig takes the niche where rust feels like overkill: tooling, game
#   engines, embedded, codec / dsp, anything interoperating heavily with c.
# - c stays where c is, and new "c-style" projects split between zig and
#   modern c (c23+).
# BUN LEAVING IS EVIDENCE FOR THIS, not against it — bun was the one entry
# on the winning list that did NOT fit that niche, and it is the one that
# left. see the case study below.


# what zig actually does differently

# 1. no hidden control flow. no operator overloading, no exceptions, no
#    destructors, no unwinding. if a function call is on the page, that's the
#    only thing happening. (rust has Drop and Deref; zig has neither.)
#    TWO OVERCLAIMS TO AVOID:
#    - zig DOES have panics. @panic is a builtin and every runtime safety
#      check panics (see point 5). the defensible claim is "no EXCEPTIONS",
#      not "no panics".
#    - zig DOES have implicit coercions: integer widening, *[N]T -> []T,
#      T -> ?T, T -> !T, comptime_int resolution. what it lacks is implicit
#      NARROWING / lossy conversion.
#    THE "NO DESTRUCTORS" PART IS A REAL TRADEOFF, NOT A FREE WIN. `defer`
#    puts the burden at every call site; rust's Drop is automatic. that is
#    precisely the cost bun cited when it left zig — see the case study.
# 2. allocators are explicit. every function that allocates takes an allocator
#    as a parameter. there's no global malloc you accidentally call. you can
#    swap in a test allocator that detects leaks, an arena allocator for
#    short-lived work, a fixed-buffer allocator with no heap at all.
# 3. `comptime` instead of macros / generics — ONE mechanism for both. no
#    separate macro language, no separate trait system. -> "macros"
# 4. first-class c interop. @cImport parses c headers directly — no ffi
#    declarations, no bindgen step — and zig compiles c and c++ itself.
#    -> "bonus ergonomic wins" for the mechanics, "where zig is winning"
#    for who uses `zig cc` without writing any zig.
# 5. no memory safety guarantees at compile time. zig has runtime safety checks
#    in debug builds (bounds, overflow, ub) but no borrow checker.
#    use-after-free is still possible.
# 6. i/o is a parameter too, as of 0.16 (Apr 2026). std.Io is passed in
#    exactly the way an allocator is, so blocking vs async vs io_uring is a
#    value you choose at the call site rather than a property baked into the
#    function. THE REAL PRINCIPLE BEHIND POINTS 2 AND 6 IS THE SAME:
#    ambient capabilities become explicit parameters.
#    THIS IS CONTESTED, and on exactly the ground point 1 claims as zig's
#    win. critics argue an Io parameter IS function coloring by another
#    name — a function taking Io can't be called from one that doesn't have
#    an Io to hand, which is viral through a different mechanism. defenders
#    counter that Io is a concrete runtime value you can store in app state
#    and swap, unlike an `async` keyword. worth knowing the argument exists
#    before repeating "no hidden control flow" as settled.


# where zig is winning

# - NOT bun. it was the flagship zig application through 1.3.x and shipped
#   as rust in 1.4 (Aug 2026) — see the case study below, it is the most
#   informative data point here.
# - ghostty (mitchell hashimoto's terminal emulator) — zig.
# - tigerbeetle (financial database) — zig, leaning on explicit-allocator for
#   predictable performance.
# - river (wayland compositor), lots of game-dev and embedded experiments.
# - NOT uxn. the reference implementation is ~150 lines of ANSI C,
#   deliberately, so it runs on a GBA or a Pico. zig ports exist; the
#   project is not zig.
# - as a c/c++ cross-compiler — `zig cc` is used by projects that aren't
#   otherwise written in zig at all (e.g. uber's go cross-compilation).


# CASE STUDY — BUN LEFT ZIG FOR RUST (Aug 2026)
# ---------------------------------------------
# bun was THE flagship zig application and is no longer written in zig. this
# is worth more than any benchmark in this file, so: the details.
#
#   bun 1.3.14    last zig release
#   bun 1.4.0     released 2026-08-20, written in rust
#   scope         535,496 lines of zig across 1,448 files
#   the port      11 days of machine time (May 3-14 2026), ~4 months end to
#                 end against a 1-year hand estimate. AI-assisted: ~50
#                 dynamic workflows, 6,502 commits. NO ZIG REMAINS in prod
#   announced     2026-07-08, shipped stable 2026-08-20
#
# WHY, in jarred sumner's own framing: "a large percentage of bugs from that
# list are use-after-free, double-free, and 'forgot to free' in an error
# path. in safe rust, these are compiler errors."
#
# the specific pain was MIXING A GC WITH MANUAL MEMORY — javascript values
# owned by a garbage collector interacting with hand-managed zig
# allocations. he calls that a rare case "no language really designs for".
#
# NOTE WHAT HE DID NOT SAY. this was not "zig is bad":
#   "zig made bun possible... i would never have been able to build this
#    much in 1 year if it wasn't for zig."
# read it as: ZIG WON THE PROTOTYPING PHASE DECISIVELY AND LOST THE
# HARDENING PHASE. that is a sharper claim than anything else in this file
# and it fits the "realistic outcome" prediction below rather than refuting
# it.
#
# TWO CONFOUNDERS, before reading too much into it:
# 1. the rewrite was only economically possible BECAUSE of AI-assisted
#    porting. nobody hand-rewrites 535k lines. so this is partly a story
#    about porting becoming cheap, not purely about zig losing.
# 2. bun's gc-plus-manual-memory problem is genuinely unusual. tigerbeetle,
#    ghostty and river don't have it, and none of them have moved.
# https://bun.com/blog/bun-v1.4
# https://bun.com/blog/bun-in-rust


# zig vs rust — the verdicts
#
# VERDICTS ONLY — mechanics live in the sections below.

# LOSES TO RUST
# - memory safety. rust proves it at compile time; zig gives tooling and
#   discipline. for kernels, browsers and parsers eating untrusted input,
#   rust is the safer pick. the bun case study above is the evidence.
# - ecosystem. crates.io has ~345k crates; zig has no central registry, so
#   there is no comparable count. the ratio is the point.
#   -> "package manager", tradeoff 2
# - stability. pre-1.0 at 0.17.0 (2026-10-02), and 0.16 -> 0.17 broke real
#   code: array `**`, `void{}`, errdefer capture, @bitCast on extern
#   structs. rust has been stable since 2015. -> tradeoff 1
# - hiring. "rust developer" is a job title; "zig developer" is a hobby
#   project.

# BEATS RUST
# - simplicity. learnable in a weekend; rust takes months.
# - compile times. significantly faster, and 0.17 brings real incremental
#   builds on x86_64-linux: zig build -fincremental --watch
# - iterating on existing c code. @cImport the header, write zig alongside,
#   link them. no rewrite. -> "bonus ergonomic wins"
# - cross-compilation. the killer feature. rust users even borrow `zig cc`
#   as their linker. -> "cross-compilation"
# - embedded. smaller binaries than rust, no runtime, no allocator unless
#   you ask for one.


# zig vs c ergonomics — point by point

# build system
# - zig replaces make / cmake / autotools with build.zig — a regular zig file
#   that describes your build. just code: variables, conditionals, functions.
#   no dsl, no separate language, no .in templates, no m4.

# cross-compilation
# - the killer feature, not exaggerated. on any host:
#     zig build -Dtarget=aarch64-linux-musl
#     zig build -Dtarget=x86_64-windows-gnu
#     zig build -Dtarget=riscv64-linux-gnu
#     zig build -Dtarget=wasm32-wasi
#   works out of the box. no toolchain install, no sysroot fiddling, no
#   CROSS_COMPILE= env vars, no docker. zig bundles libc sources (musl, glibc,
#   mingw, wasi-libc) and builds them on demand.
# - good enough that non-zig projects adopt `zig cc` purely for this —
#   see "where zig is winning".

# macros
# - zig has none. instead it has comptime:
#     fn max(comptime T: type, a: T, b: T) T {
#         return if (a > b) a else b;
#     }
# - that's the generics story AND the macro story — one mechanism. compile-time
#   code is just normal zig code the compiler runs at build time. no #define,
#   no token pasting, no preprocessor.

# package manager (as of 0.17, Oct 2026)
# - arrived in 0.11 (2023) and has NOT settled since. build.zig.zon is the
#   manifest (think Cargo.toml), dependencies fetched by url + hash, still no
#   central registry (deliberate — they want it to mature first).
# - 0.16 moved dependencies into a local zig-pkg/ directory, added a global
#   compressed cache, and added --fork for debugging a dependency in place.
# - 0.17 moved package management OUT OF THE COMPILER entirely and into the
#   build system — http, tls, git and compression now live there, built in
#   -Osafe rather than -Ofast.
# - still not as polished as cargo, but worlds better than "vendor a tarball
#   into third_party/". budget for it changing again before 1.0.


# bonus ergonomic wins

# - `zig cc` is a working c compiler — a drop-in gcc/clang replacement with
#   no zig code involved. -> "the takeaway"
# - @cImport directly parses c headers. point at stdio.h or libcurl/curl.h and
#   you get the types and functions, no ffi bindings, no bindgen.
# - defer instead of cleanup goto. `defer file.close();` runs at scope exit.
#   replaces the whole `goto cleanup;` pattern in c.
# - errors are values, not exceptions. `!T` is "T or error." compiler forces
#   you to handle them. no silent errno, no exception-tables.


# honest tradeoffs

# 1. pre-1.0. 0.17.0 as of Oct 2026. language still changes between releases —
#    usually small, occasionally not (see the 0.17 breaking list above).
#    1.0 still undated, but the 0.17 notes give a real accounting: roughly 25
#    language proposals accepted, 125 rejected, 23 undecided. the stated
#    pre-1.0 work is finishing the self-hosted backends, dropping the LLD
#    dependency, and auditing the standard library.
# 2. small ecosystem. need a json library? stdlib has one. need a postgres
#    driver? maybe. be ready to bind to c libraries (easy via @cImport, but
#    still work).
# 3. no memory safety guarantees. can still leak, use-after-free, double-free.
#    zig gives better tools — a debug allocator that catches leaks and uaf,
#    runtime bounds-checking, defined integer overflow — but no compile-time
#    proof.
#    MIND THE ALLOCATOR RENAMES:
#      GeneralPurposeAllocator -> DebugAllocator      (0.14, old name kept
#                                                      as a deprecated alias)
#      DebugAllocator          -> SafeAllocator       (0.17, adds thread-safe
#                                                      leak + corruption
#                                                      checking)
#    and 0.14 added SmpAllocator, a ~200-line singleton for ReleaseFast with
#    threads, competitive with glibc. pick by purpose: SafeAllocator to catch
#    bugs, SmpAllocator to go fast, ArenaAllocator for short-lived work,
#    FixedBufferAllocator for no heap at all.
# 4. standard library is in flux. good but still being shaped. things move,
#    and 0.17 is a fair sample: std.fmt.allocPrint(arena, ...) became
#    arena.print(...), ArrayList.getLast() became last().?, and
#    StackFallbackAllocator stopped being generic over its buffer size.


